# Part 1: Linux File System Hierarchy

## /

### Purpose

The root directory is the starting point of the Linux file system. All files and directories exist under it.

### Example Command

```bash
ls -l /
```

### Observation

Contains directories such as `/home`, `/etc`, `/var`, and `/tmp`.

### I would use this when

I need to understand the overall structure of the Linux system.

---

## /home

### Purpose

Stores personal files and directories for normal users.

### Example Command

```bash
ls -l /home
```

### Observation

Contains user home directories.

### I would use this when

I need to access user files or scripts.

---

## /root

### Purpose

Home directory of the root user.

### Example Command

```bash
ls -l /root
```

### Observation

Contains files and configurations used by the root user.

### I would use this when

I am performing administrative tasks as root.

---

## /etc

### Purpose

Stores system-wide configuration files.

### Example Command

```bash
ls -l /etc
```

### Observation

Contains files such as `hostname`, `hosts`, and service configuration files.

### I would use this when

I need to modify system or application configurations.

---

## /var/log

### Purpose

Stores application and system log files.

### Example Command

```bash
ls -l /var/log
```

### Observation

Contains log files generated by services and the operating system.

### I would use this when

I need to troubleshoot system or application issues.

---

## /tmp

### Purpose

Stores temporary files created by users and applications.

### Example Command

```bash
ls -l /tmp
```

### Observation

Contains temporary files that may be removed after reboot.

### I would use this when

I need a temporary location for testing or backups.

---

## /bin

### Purpose

Contains essential Linux commands and binaries.

### Example Command

```bash
ls -l /bin
```

### Observation

Contains commands required for basic system operation.

### I would use this when

I want to understand where important Linux commands are stored.

---

## /usr/bin

### Purpose

Contains most user-level executable commands.

### Example Command

```bash
ls -l /usr/bin
```

### Observation

Contains commands such as `git`, `vim`, and many others.

### I would use this when

I need to locate application binaries.

---

## /opt

### Purpose

Used for optional or third-party software installations.

### Example Command

```bash
ls -l /opt
```

### Observation

May contain manually installed applications.

### I would use this when

I need to manage third-party software.

---

# Hands-On Practice

## Find Largest Log Files

### Command

```bash
du -sh /var/log/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -5
```

### Purpose

Displays the largest log files and directories in `/var/log`.

---

## View Hostname Configuration

### Command

```bash
cat /etc/hostname
```

### Purpose

Displays the system hostname.

---

## Check Home Directory Contents

### Command

```bash
ls -la ~
```

### Purpose

Displays all files and directories in the user's home directory.

